item.description:
arXiv:2610.04993v1 Announce Type: new 
Abstract: For a positive integer $n$, let $$
  S_1(n)=\{n\bmod k:1\leq k\leq\lfloor n/2\rfloor\},
  \qquad s_1(n)=|S_1(n)|. $$ Baraskar and Vukusic proved $s_1(n)=\Xi n+O(n/(\log n\log\log n))$ for an explicit positive constant $\Xi$, and asked for a sharper error term. Their computations suggested that an $O(n^{1/3})$ bound might hold. We prove that, for every $\alpha>\sqrt2$, $$
  \limsup_{n\to\infty}
  \frac{s_1(n)-\Xi n}{n\exp\{-\alpha\sqrt{\log n\log\log n}\}}
  =\infty, $$ and the corresponding limit inferior is $-\infty$. In particular, no fixed power saving is possible. We also prove the complementary upper estimate $$
  s_1(n)=\Xi n+O\left(
  n\exp\{-\alpha\sqrt{\log n\log\log n}\}
  \right) $$ for every fixed $\alpha\in (0,\sqrt{2})$, sharpening the previous $O(n/(\log n\log\log n))$ bound. Thus, the constant $\sqrt2$ is the threshold for bounds of the form $n\exp\{-\alpha\sqrt{\log n\log\log n}\}$.
